{{wp|ja:カツオ}}
{{wp|Skipjack tuna}}
[[File:Katsuwonus pelamis.jpg|thumb]]

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=かつお|-}} ''katuwo''.  Found in the ''{{w|Man'yōshū}}'' of 759.<ref name="MYS">{{RQ:Manyoshu|9|1740}}, available online [https://web.archive.org/web/20190816211825/http://jti.lib.virginia.edu/japanese/manyoshu/Man9Yos.html#1740 here].  ''Katuwo'' appears spelled as {{m|ja|堅魚}}.  While this does not phonetically spell ''katuwo'' itself, given the meter of the poetry, this word must have three [[morae]], and given also [[:w:Old_Japanese#Morphophonemics|Old Japanese phonology]], we know that this cannot have been ''kata uwo'' due to the restriction on successive vowels.</ref>

{{IPAchar|/katuwo/}} → {{IPAchar|/kat͡suwo/}} → {{IPAchar|/kat͡suo/}}

Two possible derivations:
* A shift from a compound of {{com|ja|sort=かつお|堅|tr1=kata-|pos1=stem meaning "[[firm]], [[hard]]"|魚|tr2=uo|t2=[[fish]]}}, from the long-standing practice of drying lower-grade tuna into hard blocks, in part due to the common presence of parasites. A [[folk etymology]] also derives this ''[[kanji]]'' character, compare {{ja-l|木工}} → {{m|ja|杢|tr=moku||[[woodworker]]|pos=archaic}} and {{ja-l|麻呂}} → {{m|ja|麿|tr=maro|pos=[[first person|first-person]] [[pronoun]], archaic}}.<ref name="KDJc">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten Concise}}</ref>
* A shift from a compound of {{com|ja|勝つ|tr1=katsu|t1=to [[win]]|魚|tr2=uo|t2=fish}}.

Considering the rules of {{w|Old Japanese}} phonology, specifically that two vowels are not allowed in sequence, resulting in elision or fusion, the ''kata + uo'' → ''katuo'' shift seems likely.  This derivation may be partially corroborated by the rare historical synonym {{m|ja|堅魚|tr=katana}}, literally "hard fish".<ref name="KDJc"/><ref name="KDJc-katana">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten Concise|堅魚}}</ref>

The kanji spelling is an example of {{ja-r|国%訓|こっ%くん|Japanese-specific kun'yomi}} and its use may stem from the [[堅魚]] spelling.<ref>{{R:Wasei Kanji 2014|3047}}</ref>

