===Etymology===
Within Chinese, {{zh-ref|Carr (1990)}} relates it to:
* {{och-l|咢|to beat the drum}}, either because drumming was associated with dragons or because some drums were made of crocodilian skins ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).
* Words for “fear”, e.g. {{och-l|愕|startled; terrified}}, {{och-l|噩|frightening; startling}}.

Outside of Chinese, {{zh-ref|Carr (1990)}} also relates it to:
* {{cog|tai-pro||*ŋwak|mythical water creature}} (reconstruction from {{zh-ref|Chamberlain, 1977}}), which {{zh-ref|Pittayaporn (2009-2010)}}, reconstructing the root as {{m|tai-pro|*ŋɯəkᴰ||crocodile}}, holds to be a loan from Chinese.
* Proto-{{w|Austro-Tai}} {{m|und|*(m)baŋiwak||shark; crocodile}}, posited by {{w|Paul K. Benedict}} and proposed by him to give rise to the Tai word, as well as {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|鰐|わに|crocodilian; shark}}, {{ja-r|魚|うお|fish}}.

{{zh-ref|Vovin (2021)}} notes that {{och-l|鱷|tr=*ŋâk ~ *ŋɑk}} "aquatic reptile, [[saltwater crocodile]]" was attested late, for the first time in [[w:Shuowen Jiezi|Shuowen]] as {{zh-l|*𧊜}}; and that "it would be very weird if the word for a ‘saltwater crocodile’ from the middle of Huang-he (黃河) river basin that lies quite far away from both tropics and the sea would be borrowed by Tai languages in Southern China". Therefore, Vovin contends that, instead, {{och-l|鱷|tr=*ŋâk ~ *ŋɑk}} had been borrowed into [[w:Eastern Han Chinese|Later Han Chinese]] from {{bor|zh|tai}}.

